TL;DR: A tunable NM-ROM for elliptic and parabolic PDEs that exposes a deployment-time accuracy/speed Pareto curve from a single trained model, matching neural-operator accuracy and reaching up to 324x over conjugate gradient.
Abstract:
Neural operators such as Fourier Neural Operators and DeepONets deliver one (accuracy, speed) point per trained model and offer no way to tune either at deployment. We present a non-linear manifold reduced order model (NM-ROM) for elliptic and parabolic PDEs that exposes a continuous accuracy/speed Pareto frontier from a single trained decoder, controlled at inference time by three solver-side knobs: the Gauss-Newton iteration cap, the Gauss-Newton tolerance, and the empirical-quadrature (EQ) sample count. The framework combines a matrix-free Galerkin projection of the discrete PDE residual onto a learned manifold, evaluated through JAX forward- and reverse-mode autodiff; NNLS-based EQ hyper-reduction that decouples residual cost from mesh size; exact boundary condition enforcement by a binary decoder mask; and a decoder architecture --- a Vision Transformer encoder feeding a LinearCPDecoder (linear skip + shallow MLP + canonical-polyadic spatial head) --- that we identified by sweeping decoder families against three constraints: cold-start latent Gauss-Newton convergence, EQ compatibility, and sub-cubic parameter scaling in mesh side. Across Poisson and Heat in 2D and 3D at multiple resolutions, the trained-once Pareto frontier matches or beats FNO, DeepONet, and the SMA-NM-ROM baseline on accuracy in most cells and is strictly faster than full-order conjugate gradient, reaching up to 324x wall-clock speedup in some cases.

