Summary:
This paper proposes a nonlinear manifold reduced-order model (NM-ROM) for solving two- and three-dimensional elliptic and parabolic PDEs. The method first learns a low-dimensional nonlinear solution manifold of full-order solutions using a ViT encoder and a LinearCPDecoder, and then solves the discretized PDE by projecting the residual onto the tangent space of the learned manifold via Galerkin projection. The decoder enforces Dirichlet boundary conditions exactly through a binary mask and supports evaluation only at a small subset of empirically selected quadrature nodes via a CP tensor spatial head. The framework further adopts NNLS-based empirical quadrature for hyper-reduction and leverages JAX JVP/VJP primitives to implement matrix-free projected operators. The central claim of the paper is that, after training, multiple accuracy-speed operating points can be obtained without retraining by simply adjusting the Gauss-Newton iteration limit, convergence tolerance, and the number of empirical quadrature samples during inference. Experiments are conducted on 2D and 3D Poisson and Heat equations, with comparisons against FNO, DeepONet, SMA-NM-ROM, and full-order conjugate gradient solvers.

Contribution Type: General: Most submissions will fall into this type.
Strengths And Weaknesses:
Quality
The overall framework is well engineered. Combining exact boundary-condition enforcement, tangent-space Galerkin projection, empirical quadrature, automatic differentiation, and a node-wise CP decoder constitutes a technically reasonable and practically motivated design. In particular, the binary masking strictly satisfies Dirichlet boundary conditions, the CP spatial head avoids the parameter explosion of dense decoders in 3D, and JAX JVP/VJP naturally enables matrix-free evaluation without explicitly constructing high-dimensional Jacobians.

However, the current version contains several serious issues that directly undermine its main conclusions.

First, the paper's central claim—that a single trained model produces an entire Pareto frontier—is contradicted by its own tables and appendix. In Table 2, the best-accuracy and best-speed operating points often use different latent dimensions or CP ranks. For example, Poisson-3D (N=32) uses different values of (k) (24/8) and (R) (512/128), while Poisson-2D (N=128) and (N=256) also use different CP ranks. Changing (k) or (R) changes the model architecture and parameter tensor dimensions, making it impossible for these operating points to originate from the same decoder. Appendix A further states that the two operating points for Poisson-3D (N=128) were obtained using different training recipes, involving different training and fine-tuning procedures. This directly contradicts the repeated claim in the main paper that only inference-time solver knobs are changed without retraining.

The paper also contains internal inconsistencies in the reported numerical results. For example, the best-speed relative error for Poisson-3D (N=64) is reported as (5.88\times10^{-2}) in Table 1 but (7.69\times10^{-3}) in Table 2, despite both corresponding to the same reported 130× speedup. Furthermore, the authors claim Pareto dominance over all baselines except Poisson-2D (N=64), yet on Heat-3D (N=32), FNO achieves a lower error ((8.17\times10^{-2})) than the best-accuracy NM-ROM ((9.30\times10^{-2})), meaning this case also fails to satisfy the stated Pareto dominance criterion. These are not minor typographical mistakes but contradictions that directly affect the headline claims of the paper.

Second, the derivation of the latent-space time discretization for the heat equation is incomplete and, according to the presented equations, does not appear valid for nonlinear decoders. For a general nonlinear manifold, a consistent backward Euler discretization should satisfy

 

which is a nonlinear equation in (z_{n+1}). Equation (4), however, directly presents a linear system in (z_{n+1}) by effectively replacing (\tilde u(z)) with (J_D z), omitting the decoder bias, current operating point, and linearization remainder. Such a formulation is generally valid only for homogeneous linear decoders unless one explicitly derives a frozen-Jacobian approximation together with the corresponding correction term on the right-hand side. Moreover, while the methodology section states that each heat-equation time step is solved only with CG, the appendix and hyperparameter tables state that the Heat experiments move along the speed axis by varying the Gauss–Newton iteration limit and tolerance, resulting in inconsistent algorithmic descriptions.

The empirical quadrature component also lacks key algorithmic details needed to support the claimed deployment-time tunability. Standard NNLS in Eq. (5) returns a support set determined by optimization, yet the paper never explains how an arbitrary target sample count is specified, how nested quadrature rules are constructed, whether NNLS is recomputed after reducing the number of quadrature nodes, or whether different quadrature sizes require additional offline optimization. The appendix specifies a minimum support size of 300 nodes, whereas Tables 3 and 4 list (n_{\rm eq}\in{4,\ldots,64}), and Table 2 reports actual values of (N_{\rm eq}) between 512 and 2560. These three definitions cannot be reconciled. Furthermore, once row weights are introduced, the projected operator generally takes the form (J^\top W KJ), which is not guaranteed to remain symmetric. Nevertheless, the paper directly applies conjugate gradient without explaining why symmetry and positive definiteness are preserved.

The experimental comparisons are also insufficient to support the reported order-of-magnitude speedups. Structured constant-coefficient Poisson and Heat equations admit highly optimized FFT/DST solvers, geometric multigrid, algebraic multigrid, and preconditioned Krylov methods, yet the paper compares only against an unpreconditioned CG solver. For the heat equation, one may additionally reuse factorizations or preconditioners across time steps. Consequently, a reported 100–300× speedup over plain CG does not demonstrate superiority over competitive PDE solvers. Meanwhile, the proposed method receives extensive per-cell tuning of architecture, CP rank, training schedule, fine-tuning strategy, and random seed selection, whereas FNO and DeepONet are evaluated only using fixed canonical configurations without problem-specific tuning. DeepONet diverges on every Heat benchmark, and FNO even becomes slower than CG in 3D. These observations suggest that the baseline implementations or training protocols should be investigated more carefully rather than being interpreted as evidence of universal Pareto superiority.

Because one of the paper's main contributions concerns JAX compilation, matrix-free implementation, and wall-clock performance, the timing methodology is particularly important. However, the paper does not specify whether JIT compilation time is included, whether GPU synchronization is enforced, the number of warm-up runs, repeated measurements, numerical precision, or timing variance. The authors further claim that simply treating (\kappa) as a runtime JAX argument improves the Heat-3D (N=128) speedup from 14.7× to 191×, suggesting that the reported results may be highly sensitive to recompilation or tracing overhead. Since neither anonymous code nor checkpoints are provided, these large speedup claims are difficult to verify independently.

Clarity
The paper is generally well organized. The motivation, overall methodology, and architectural design are easy to follow, and Figure 1 provides a clear overview of the training and inference pipeline. However, several critical implementation details required for reproducibility are missing, particularly regarding how empirical quadrature sample counts become deployment-time knobs, whether the Heat solver actually uses CG or Gauss–Newton iterations, and whether different Pareto operating points genuinely share the same trained checkpoint.

Significance
Introducing deployment-time computational budget control into learned-manifold ROMs is an interesting and practically relevant problem. For applications requiring repeated PDE solves while maintaining residual-based physical consistency, the proposed framework could serve as a useful compromise between neural operators and traditional reduced-order models. The combination of node-wise CP decoding with hyper-reduction may also prove valuable for large-scale 3D simulations.

Originality
The main novelty lies in the system-level integration rather than in the individual components. Nonlinear manifold ROMs, Galerkin/LSPG projection, empirical quadrature, automatic differentiation, hard boundary-condition enforcement, ViTs, and CP tensor decompositions are all established techniques. The combination of a linear skip connection, a shallow MLP, and a CP spatial head under the joint design constraints of cold-start convergence, sparse node evaluation, and scalable 3D parameterization represents a meaningful engineering contribution.

