1. Single trained model. We thank the reviewer for carefully identifying these inconsistencies. In 4 of 11 cells the two operating points used different latent dimensions, CP ranks, or training recipes (Poisson-3D N=32 used k=24/8 and R=512/128; Poisson-2D N=128 and N=256 used different CP ranks; Poisson-3D N=128 used a separate fine-tuning recipe). We initially showed that to show the best possible results achievable by this method however we have updated it now. All four cells were re-trained. One architecture and one checkpoint per cell now produce both operating points, and the only quantities that change between the two columns are the Gauss-Newton iteration cap, the tolerance, and the quadrature set. The revised Table 1 lists one architecture per row, without slashed entries.

2. Internal inconsistencies. We thank the reviewer for carefully identifying these inconsistencies. The conflicting Poisson-3D N=64 numbers (5.88e-2 vs 7.69e-3) are replaced by one consistent pair from the re-run. The bold on Heat-3D N=32 was wrong; FNO is more accurate there (8.17e-2 vs 9.30e-2) and the revised table reflects it. All tables are now generated by a single script from the released result files, which rules out this class of error.

3. Eq. (4). We thank the reviewer for pointing this out: as printed, the equation is only valid for a homogeneous linear decoder. Our implementation never used it; it solves the full nonlinear time-step equation. The issue lies in the presentation of the algorithm in the manuscript rather than in the implementation. We have revised the corresponding passage to accurately reflect the implementation:

A backward-Euler step of size Δt on the manifold requires the fully discrete, nonlinear residual R_n(z) := ũ(z) + Δt κ K ũ(z) − ũ(z_n), whose least-squares solution z_{n+1} = argmin ‖R_n(z)‖ defines the step (an LSPG-type condition; for a linear decoder it coincides with the Galerkin root). We compute it at every time step by a damped Gauss-Newton iteration on R_n, warm-started at z_n.

This also clears up the CG-vs-Gauss-Newton inconsistency you noticed. The heat solver moves along the speed axis through the Gauss-Newton cap and tolerance; the sentence claiming each step is solved with CG was wrong and is removed. Prompted by this comment we also stress-tested our released heat code and found a real bug: the rollout stored the wrong quantity as the next right-hand side, which silently froze the recursion (a 50-step rollout agreed with a 1-step rollout to 6e-8). Fixing it changed the error of the released port by roughly 40x. We verified that the pipeline which produced the submitted numbers does not contain the bug; both the bug and the verification are documented in the revision.

4. Quadrature counts; CG on the projected operator. The three counts that could not be reconciled were three different quantities the paper never separated: the target node count, the achieved node count after NNLS, and the number of residual snapshots used to assemble the selection problem (the small n_eq values in Tables 3-4). Revised text:

(i) Requested vs. achieved counts: the pipeline takes a target count m_eq; when the NNLS support is smaller than m_eq, the node set is padded with the top-ranked remaining nodes by NNLS weight until N_eq = m_eq. All achieved counts ship in the released result files.

Selection happens entirely offline; at deployment we only choose among pre-computed sets. On symmetry: no CG is applied to the projected operator. Each Gauss-Newton step solves a small k-by-k system directly, which is well-posed regardless of the row weights.

5. Classical solvers and baseline tuning. We agree on both. The table carries Jacobi-CG and ILU columns, we withdraw the 324x claim (a direct solve takes milliseconds on these cells), and the corrected speedups against a correctly converging CG are 1.1-26x depending on the accuracy setting. FNO is tuned per cell with the official neuraloperator library (8 configurations per cell), and the table reports where it wins: it is more accurate than us on Poisson-3D N=32 and N=64 and on Heat-3D N=32. DeepONet was replaced by POD-DeepONet using the DeepONet authors' implementation (DeepXDE), tuned per cell; plain DeepONet fails because its autoregressive rollout amplifies one-step errors until the state overflows, which is a structural failure mode rather than a bug in our setup, so re-tuning would not have fixed it.

6. Timing methodology. A new appendix specifies the protocol:

(i) Warm-up: every JIT-compiled kernel is called once on representative inputs and the result discarded, so compilation never contaminates a measurement. (ii) Synchronisation: each measured region is bracketed by device synchronisation. (iii) Aggregation: we report the median over repeated solves; per-case values ship in the released result files. (iv) Isolation: quoted timings come from an otherwise idle, dedicated device. (v) Like-for-like: ROM and FOM run in the same framework and precision for every speedup ratio.

We thank the reviewer for pointing out this issue. The observed κ effect was due to a compilation-caching artifact, which is now explicitly documented in the appendix. We have not yet re-timed the heat block under this protocol (footnote (b) flags its speedups as submission-era); that will submit the results after retiming within the next few days.

