Thank you for the review. You asked for three things: a precise statement of what we solve, harder PDEs, and comparisons against real solvers. We have run new experiments for all three, and answer in that order.

1. What we solve, precisely. We describe the problem setup in Section 5 of the current manuscript, but we will revise the presentation to improve its clarity. Poisson: −Δu = f on the unit square/cube with homogeneous Dirichlet boundary conditions, second-order finite differences; the right-hand side is f(x) = 10 sin(k₁πx) sin(k₂πy) (· sin(k₃πz)) with frequencies drawn uniformly from [1, 3] per instance; 700 training and 140 test instances. Heat: ∂u/∂t = κΔu on the same domains with zero boundary values, initial conditions of one to three random Gaussian bumps, κ drawn log-uniformly from [0.01, 0.5], 50 implicit time steps. Burgers: viscous, ν in [1e-3, 1e-2], safeguarded Newton in the full solver. Wave: Crank-Nicolson on the first-order system. The Poisson ground truth is now the verified discrete solution rather than an analytic target field, since the latter does not satisfy the Dirichlet condition exactly at non-integer frequencies; all Poisson data was regenerated accordingly, for our method and every baseline.

2. Harder problems. To address this concern, we added viscous Burgers (1D and 2D, advection-dominated, ground truth verified against an independent implementation) and the 2D wave equation (no dissipation, so projection errors do not decay). We also added the linear POD-Galerkin control these problems call for, with the same hyper-reduction machinery and rank k swept:

