Neural-operator inference is a single forward pass, so FNO's speedup over an implicit rollout is bounded only by its accuracy; on Burgers it is far faster than us at comparable error, while on the wave equation it stays near 22% error at every resolution and we reach 0.5-1.6%, with our speedup growing from about 1x at N=64 to 15x at N=256. One clarification on what produces this: on Burgers-2D and wave our configuration is a frozen POD-type basis with a damped least-squares Gauss-Newton rollout, so the stability gain there comes from the solve formulation rather than from decoder nonlinearity; the learned decoder is used on Poisson, heat, and Burgers-1D. Higher-resolution Burgers runs (2D N=128, 1D N=512) are currently running and we will report back as soon as we have some updated results.

3. Real solver comparisons. To address your concern, we added Jacobi-preconditioned CG and ILU (paired with BiCGStab, since ILU factors are not symmetric), and timed direct solvers as well. Representative Poisson figures, per solve: N=64, 3.4 ms (Jacobi-CG) and 9.9 ms (ILU); N=256, 519 ms and 2996 ms; 3D N=128, 14.5 s and 52.6 s. A fast direct solve finishes the constant-coefficient cells in milliseconds. We therefore withdraw the 324x claim: it was measured against an unpreconditioned CG reference and was misleading. Our corrected speedups against a correctly converging CG are 1.1-26x depending on the accuracy setting, and the case for a reduced model rests on the harder problems above, where no direct solver applies.

On conditioning. The conditioning of the operator is indeed an important factor when interpreting speedup comparisons is. That is part of why the elliptic cells are the weakest evidence in the paper: they are well-conditioned and constant-coefficient, so classical methods are extremely strong there. The advection-dominated problems above are where the comparison is informative.

On a domain with a corner. You asked specifically for a less well-conditioned case, such as Poisson on a domain with a corner, and we have not run that yet. We agree it is the right test. We are currently setting up an L-shaped Poisson cell. If the experiments are completed during the discussion period, we will post the results in this thread.

Why these problems were absent from the submission. There was no technical obstacle; the method ported to Burgers and the wave equation without modification. We simply tested the problems the pipeline was first built on, which, as you point out, is where the comparison means the least. The harder problems are now central to the paper.

