Summary:
The paper presents an intrusive non-linear manifold reduced-order model (NM-ROM) for the Poisson and Heat equations in 2D and 3D. A learned decoder defines the solution manifold (with Dirichlet boundary conditions enforced exactly by masking), the discrete PDE residual is solved on this manifold by Galerkin projection, and empirical quadrature plus a matrix-free JAX implementation keep the cost independent of the full mesh size. Its main contribution is that a single trained model exposes a tunable accuracy/speed frontier at inference via three solver-side knobs, rather than the single fixed operating point of a neural operator. The paper reports 12 to 324 times speedups over full-order conjugate gradient and claims Pareto-dominance over FNO, DeepONet, and SMA-NM-ROM on ten of eleven cells.

Contribution Type: General: Most submissions will fall into this type.
Strengths And Weaknesses:
Strengths.

The paper presents a coherent systems contribution: a learned nonlinear manifold, Galerkin projection, empirical-quadrature hyper-reduction, and matrix-free implementation are integrated into a single reduced-order solver.

The most convincing technical ideas are the linear skip connection, which stabilizes cold-start latent Gauss--Newton solves, and the CP-based decoder, which enables efficient pointwise evaluation and makes hyper-reduction practical in 3D.

The matrix-free JAX implementation is technically sound.

Weaknesses.

The empirical evidence does not currently support the paper's strongest claim. The headline "Pareto-dominance over neural operators (
 cells)" rests on baselines that are broken, untuned, or run as oracles: DeepONet fails catastrophically on every cell -- rel-
 on Poisson-2D, 
 on Poisson-3D, and non-finite / 
 on Heat. This is a non-functional baseline, not a competitive one. SMA-NM-ROM is reported in oracle warm-start or it OOMs / explodes; the fair cold-start protocol is invoked only to motivate the authors' own design. FNO is not tuned per cell (width 
). Single seed, no variance reported; Heat-3D accuracy is poor (rel-
 
--
) and degrades from 
 to 
. The comparison is intrusive vs non-intrusive (NM-ROM uses 
 and 
; the operators do not), so an accuracy/speed head-to-head is not apples-to-apples. The natural intrusive baseline (linear POD-Galerkin + DEIM with the same knobs) is present in the paper but not foregrounded.

The paper does not demonstrate when a non-linear manifold is actually needed. The evaluation is restricted to diffusion-dominated PDEs (Poisson, Heat) -- precisely the setting where low-dimensional linear subspaces are already known to perform well. The paper therefore never exhibits a regime where the non-linear manifold is the enabling ingredient rather than an expensive alternative to linear POD. Relatedly, the method uses Galerkin projection rather than LSPG; Galerkin is known to be unstable for non-symmetric / advection-dominated operators, but this is neither analyzed nor discussed.

Originality is incremental. No component is fundamentally new. A decoder supporting per-node evaluation for hyper-reduction is already the design principle of SMA-NM-ROM and DD-NM-ROM; the new elements are only the CP head and the linear skip. The "deployment-time tunable frontier" is inherent to any solver-based ROM -- Newton/Krylov tolerances and EQ count are standard accuracy/cost knobs -- and is novel only relative to neural operators.

Theoretical guarantees. I do not require a full convergence theory for a systems/methods paper, but the work would be significantly strengthened by some analytical justification of the cold-start GN procedure, the EQ approximation error as a function of 
