On 1D Burgers the linear ROM reaches high accuracy only at basis sizes where it is slower than the full-order model. On 2D Burgers it is unstable at every rank small enough to give a speedup. On the wave equation it is inaccurate at N≥128 and slower than the FOM at N=64. Our solver is stable and accurate on the same problems (see the table above). It matters for your question which part is doing the work, so to be clear: on Burgers-2D and wave our configuration is a frozen POD-type basis with a damped least-squares Gauss-Newton rollout, so the stability gain there comes from the solve formulation, not from decoder nonlinearity; the learned decoder is used on Poisson, heat, and Burgers-1D. On Galerkin vs LSPG, the rule we now state and support is: exact Galerkin projection when the manifold is linear, and a damped least-squares (LSPG-type) Gauss-Newton solve otherwise, which is what stabilises the advection-dominated rollouts. Our wave solver's rollout error is within 1.02-1.24x of the best achievable on its basis, so the solver adds little beyond the basis error. On the smooth elliptic cells we agree with your expectation: linear reduction is very strong there, and the revision includes the full linear-ROM comparison on Poisson and heat.

Q4. Seeds, and is the frontier free from one model? Burgers-1D over three training seeds: 0.55%, 0.64%, 0.59%. The wave and 2D-Burgers configurations use deterministic bases, so there is no training randomness to report on those cells. Quadrature sets are selected once, offline; at deployment we choose among pre-computed sets and change the Gauss-Newton cap and tolerance, and nothing is re-optimised at test time. While checking this we also found and fixed a defect in how padded quadrature points were weighted, now documented in the revision.

Still to come, and we will submit it here. Three items from your review are not finished, and we will add them to this thread as they land during the discussion period: the SMA-NM-ROM cold-start runs (Q2); the matched learned-vs-frozen-chart ablation on an advection-dominated problem (Q3); and seed variance on the genuinely borderline cell, Heat-3D N=32, where the margin against FNO is 8.17e-2 vs 9.30e-2.

Your point on theory is also fair. We do not have convergence guarantees for the cold-start Gauss-Newton procedure or a bound on the quadrature error as a function of N_eq for unseen instances. We have updated the revision to state that these open questions in the limitations.

On originality and the intrusive asymmetry. We accept the characterisation and have aligned the claims: the contribution is the working combination plus the empirical findings, with the frontier claim made only relative to neural operators. The asymmetry is now stated in the introduction before any comparison; revised text:

NM-ROMs are intrusive: they require access to the discrete system at inference. FNO and DeepONet are non-intrusive maps trained purely on data. The trade-off differs accordingly. The intrusive route gives residual consistency (an equation is actually solved, so errors can be checked and reduced), while the non-intrusive route applies even when no solver is available. Our comparisons ask a single question: accuracy per wall-clock at deployment, given that a discretisation exists. Every baseline is evaluated under that condition.

